Q1-> Write a fn where you can merge two strings alternatively?

Ans-> function merge(word1,word2){

let res = "";
let max = Math.max(word1.length, word2.length);

for(let i=0;i<max;i++){
    if(i<word1.length){
        res += word1[i];
    }
    if(i<word2.length){
        res += word2[i];
    }
}

return res;

}

Aapne string concatenation (res += ...) ka use kiya hai. JavaScript me strings immutable (unchangeable) hoti hain. Jab bhi aap res += word1[i] karte hain, JavaScript background me ek nayi string banata hai aur purani string ko copy karta hai.Isse kya hota hai? Choti strings ke liye ("abc", "pqr") isse koi farak nahi padta. Lekin agar strings bohot badi hoñ (jaise lakhs of characters), toh iski time complexity badh kar $O((N + M)^2)$ ho sakti hai kyunki har iteration me string copying hoti hai.Best Practice: Isiliye arrays ka use karke .join('') karna (jaisa humne pehle discuss kiya tha) ya fir JavaScript me arrays ko mutate karna zyada optimal mana jata hai for large inputs.

function mergeAlternately(word1, word2) {
    let result = [];
    let maxLength = Math.max(word1.length, word2.length);
    
    for (let i = 0; i < maxLength; i++) {
        if (i < word1.length) {
            result.push(word1[i]);
        }
        if (i < word2.length) {
            result.push(word2[i]);
        }
    }
    
    return result.join('');
}

// Example usage:
console.log(mergeAlternately("abc", "pqr")); // Output: "apbqcr"
console.log(mergeAlternately("ab", "pqrs")); // Output: "apbqrs"


Q2-> Write a function to return the longest word in a sentence?

Ans-> function findLongestWord(sentence){

if(!sentence || sentence.trim() === "") return "";

// Sentence ko spaces ke basis par words ke array me convert karo

const words = sentence.split(" ");
let longestWord = "";

for(let word of words){
    if(word.length>longestWord.length){
        longestWord = word;
    }
}
return longestWord;

}

// Example usage:
console.log(findLongestWord("The quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog")); // Output: "jumped"
console.log(findLongestWord("Web development is awesome")); // Output: "development"

Q3-> What will be the output of console.log(2 - "2")? Also, what will be the output of console.log(2 + "2")? So, we have to give the output of two console.log. First one is 2 - "2". Second one is 2 + "2".

Ans->. In dono ke outputs yeh honge:
console.log(2 - "2"); Output: 0
console.log(2 + "2"); Output: "22"

Phla case (2 - "2"):
Minus (-) operator string ke sath use nahi hota (kyunki minus ka matlab math subtraction hota hai). Isliye JavaScript Implicit Type Coercion karta hai aur string "2" ko number 2 me convert kar deta hai. Phir operation hota hai: 2 - 2, jiska result 0 aata hai.

Dusra case (2 + "2"):
Plus (+) operator ke do kaam hote hain—Addition (numbers ke liye) aur Concatenation (strings ko jodna). Jab ek operand string ho ("2"), toh JavaScript baaki sabhi operands ko bhi string bana deta hai aur unhe jod deta hai. Isko string concatenation kehte hain. Isliye number 2 string "2" ban jata hai aur result "22" (string) aata hai.

🎯 Summary:
Minus (-) hamesha values ko number banata hai (position matter nahi karti).

Plus (+) agar ek bhi jagah string dekh le, toh sabko string bana deta hai (position matter nahi karti).

Q4-> let arr = [[1,2] , [3,4] , [5,6] , [7,8]] Merge it into a single array do not use builtin function?

Ans-> function merge(arr){

    let res = [];

for(let i=0;i<arr.length;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<arr[i].length;j++){
        res.push(arr[i][j]);
    }
}
return res;
}

⏱️ Complexity Analysis (Bonus Points)
Time Complexity: O(N), jahan N poore nested array ke total elements ki count hai (yahan 8 elements hain). Hum har ek element ko exactly ek baar visit kar rahe hain.
Space Complexity: O(N), kyunki hum ek naya result array return kar rahe hain jisme saare elements store ho rahe hain.


Q5-> What do you mean by infinite currying? Give its example. Explain it in a very, very simplest way with example.

Ans->Normal functions me hum saare arguments ek hi baar me pass karte hain, jaise add(1, 2, 3).
Lekin Currying me hum function ko tod dete hain taaki hum ek-ek karke arguments pass kar sakein, jaise: add(1)(2)(3).

Aur Infinite Currying ka matlab hai ki aap function ko lagataar (infinitely) chain karke jitne marzi chahein arguments pass karte ja sakte hain, jab tak aap rokna na chahein!

function add(a) {
    return function(b) {
        if (b !== undefined) {
            return add(a + b); // Agar agla number mila, toh jod kar dobara function return karo
        }
        return a; // Agar aur number nahi mila (khali bracket), toh total sum return kar do
    };
}

// Kaise use karein:
console.log(add(1)(2)(3)()); // Output: 6
console.log(add(5)(10)(15)(20)()); // Output: 50

Q7-> Diff between setTimeout and setInterval?

Ans-> In dono me main farak yeh hai ki setTimeout ek baar chal kar ruk jata hai, jabki setInterval baar-baar (repeatedly) chalta rehta hai jab tak aap usko manually rokein nahi.

Aaiye inko detail me samajhte hain:

1. setTimeout (Ek Baar ke liye)
Kaam: Yeh specify kiye gaye time (milliseconds) ke baad function ko sirf ek baar execute karta hai.

Real-life analogy: Sochiye aapne subah uthne ke liye 6 baje ka ek single alarm lagaya. Alarm bajegea, aap uthenge, aur kaam khatam.

// Yeh 2 seconds (2000ms) baad sirf ek baar chalega
const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
    console.log("Yeh 2 second baad print hoga!");
}, 2000);

// Agar beech me hi isko cancel karna ho:
clearTimeout(timeoutId);

2. setInterval (Baar-baar repetition ke liye)
Kaam: Yeh function ko har ek fixed time interval ke baad repeatedly (loop ki tarah) call karta rehta hai.

Real-life analogy: Sochiye aapne stopwatch ya reminder lagaya hai jo har 5 minute baad ring karega, jab tak aap khud usko off na kar dein.

let count = 0;

// Yeh har 1 second (1000ms) baad baar-baar chalega
const intervalId = setInterval(() => {
    count++;
    console.log(`Count: ${count}`);
    
    // Agar 5 baar chalne ke baad rokna ho:
    if (count === 5) {
        clearInterval(intervalId);
        console.log("Interval stopped!");
    }
}, 1000);

Q8-> arr = [1,2,3,4,1,2] return the duplicates in an array?

Ans-> function findDuplicates(arr){
    let seen = [];
    let duplicates = [];

for(let i=0;i<arr.length;i++){
    let num = arr[i];

    if(seen.includes(num)){
        // Agar haan, toh kya yeh 'duplicates' list me pehle se hai?
        if(!duplicates.include(num)){
            duplicates.push(num); // Agar nahi hai, toh duplicate maan kar add kar lo
        }
    }
    else{
        seen.push(num)
    }
}
    return duplicates;
}

// Test case:
const input = [1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2];
console.log(findDuplicates(input)); 
// Output: [1, 2]

Q9-> What difference between client side JavaScript and server side JavaScript?

1. Client-Side JavaScript (Frontend)
Client-side ka matlab hai woh JavaScript jo user ke browser (jaise Chrome, Safari, Firefox) ke andar run hoti hai.

Kaam Kya Hai: Iska main kaam webpage ko interactive banana, animations dikhana, form validation karna, aur user ke actions (clicks, typing) par response dena hota hai.

Environment: Yeh browser ke JavaScript Engine (jaise V8 in Chrome) par chalti hai.

Kya Access Kar Sakti Hai: Iske paas DOM (Document Object Model), window object, aur browser ki APIs (jaise localStorage, geolocation) ka access hota hai.

Example: Ek button click karne par modal pop-up khulna ya form me email ka format check karna.

2. Server-Side JavaScript (Backend)
Server-side ka matlab hai woh JavaScript jo remote server par run hoti hai (browser ke andar nahi). Iske liye sabse popular platform Node.js hai.

Kaam Kya Hai: Iska main kaam databases ke sath baat karna, user ki authentication (login/signup) handle karna, API endpoints banana, aur business logic ko manage karna hota hai.

Environment: Yeh server par standalone environment me chalti hai (jaise Node.js runtime).

Kya Access Kar Sakti Hai: Iske paas file system (fs module), database drivers, aur server ki resources ka access hota hai. Lekin iske paas DOM ya window object nahi hota (kyunki wahan koi browser screen hi nahi hai!).

Example: User ne login button dabaya, toh server-side code database me check karega ki password sahi hai ya nahi aur response bhejega.

Q10-> what is object destructuring? Give its example.?

Ans-> Object Destructuring ka matlab hota hai kisi object me se properties (keys) ko bahar nikal kar alag-alag variables me store karna, wo bhi bohot hi chote aur clean syntax me.

Pehle hume object ki properties access karne ke liye baar-baar user.name, user.age likhna padta tha. Destructuring ki madad se hum ek hi line me unhe variables bana lete hain.

const user = {
    name: "Aniket",
    age: 24,
    profession: "Developer"
};

// --- Destructuring syntax ---
const { name, age, profession } = user;

console.log(name);       // Output: "Aniket"
console.log(age);        // Output: 24
console.log(profession); // Output: "Developer"

Q11-> Is JavaScript dynamically typed or statically typed? Why? Explain.

Ans-> JavaScript ek dynamically typed language hai.

Iska seedha sa matlab yeh hai ki jab aap JavaScript me koi variable banate hain, toh aapko yeh batane ki zaroorat nahi padti ki us variable me kis tarah ka data aayega (jaise string, number, ya boolean). JavaScript khud-b-khud runtime par (code chalte waqt) type ko pehchan leta hai, aur zaroorat padne par variable ka type badal bhi sakta hai.


